window.__labLoaded({"id":"9.2","title":"Assumptions and Normality: Can I Trust This Test?","goal":"Check normality with the Shapiro-Wilk test and a normal Q-Q plot for two samples of 20 values from the lesson, one roughly normal (exam scores) and one skewed (response times).","question":"Two researchers each have 20 measurements and want to run a t-test, which assumes the data come from something close to a normal (bell) curve. One has exam scores; the other has response times in seconds. Is the normal assumption believable for each sample, and how can we tell by both a test and a picture?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the two samples","text":"Exam scores look roughly bell-shaped. Response times have a long tail of a few very slow values. A look at the mean versus the median already hints at the difference.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nexam = np.array([62, 65, 68, 70, 71, 72, 73, 74, 75, 76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 82, 83, 85, 87, 90, 94])\nresp = np.array([1.2, 1.4, 1.5, 1.7, 1.9, 2.0, 2.2, 2.3, 2.6, 2.8, 3.1, 3.5, 3.9, 4.6, 5.4, 6.8, 8.9, 12.5, 18.7, 31.2])\n\nfor name, x in [('exam scores', exam), ('response times', resp)]:\n    print(f'{name:15s} mean = {x.mean():.2f}, median = {np.median(x):.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for arrays of numbers.","Load matplotlib's plotting tools as plt, for the Q-Q plots.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which contain the Shapiro-Wilk test and the Q-Q plot.","Store the 20 exam scores as an array.","Store the 20 response times in seconds. A few are far larger than the rest.","Loop over both samples, each paired with a name, so we write the summary only once.","Print the mean and median of each. For a bell-shaped sample they are close; a big gap hints at skew."],"meaning":"Exam scores have a mean of 77.05 and a median of 76.50, almost the same, as in a symmetric bell shape. Response times have a mean of 5.91 but a median of only 2.95: a few very slow responses drag the mean up, a sign of right skew.","out":"exam scores     mean = 77.05, median = 76.50\nresponse times  mean = 5.91, median = 2.95","figs":[]},{"title":"Shapiro-Wilk test of normality","text":"Shapiro-Wilk starts from the assumption that the data are normal. A small p-value (below 0.05) is evidence against normality; a large one only means we found no evidence against it.","code":"for name, x in [('exam scores', exam), ('response times', resp)]:\n    w = stats.shapiro(x)\n    p_text = f'{w.pvalue:.4f}' if w.pvalue >= 0.0001 else '< 0.0001'\n    verdict = 'no evidence against normality' if w.pvalue >= 0.05 else 'evidence of non-normality'\n    print(f'{name:15s} W = {w.statistic:.3f}, p = {p_text}  ->  {verdict}')","explain":["Loop over the same two samples.","shapiro runs the Shapiro-Wilk test of normality and returns W (near 1 means close to normal) and a p-value.","Format the p-value for printing, and write '< 0.0001' when it is too small to show with 4 decimals.","Read the verdict from the p-value: below 0.05 is evidence against normality; above is no evidence against it.","Print the name, W, the p-value and the verdict on one line."],"meaning":"Exam scores: W = 0.993, p = 0.9998. There is no evidence against normality (that is not proof of normality, just no sign of trouble). Response times: W = 0.641, p < 0.0001, strong evidence that they are not normal. The two samples are clearly different.","out":"exam scores     W = 0.993, p = 0.9998  ->  no evidence against normality\nresponse times  W = 0.641, p = < 0.0001  ->  evidence of non-normality","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the normal Q-Q plots","text":"A Q-Q plot lines up the sorted data against what a normal sample would give. Points close to the straight line mean roughly normal; a curve that bends away, as with the response times, signals skew.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.subplot(1, 2, 1)\nstats.probplot(exam, dist='norm', plot=plt)\nplt.title('Exam scores')\nplt.subplot(1, 2, 2)\nstats.probplot(resp, dist='norm', plot=plt)\nplt.title('Response times')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","explain":["Start a new figure that fits the page.","Make the first of two side-by-side panels (1 row, 2 columns).","Draw the exam scores' normal Q-Q plot: sorted data against what a normal sample would give, plus a reference line.","Title the left panel.","Switch to the second panel.","Draw the same Q-Q plot for the response times.","Title the right panel.","Tidy the spacing so the panels do not overlap.","Show the figure."],"meaning":"On the left the exam-score dots hug the straight line, so they look roughly normal. On the right the response times bend away, with the largest values far above the line. That upward bend is the signature of a long right tail (skew). The picture and the test agree.","out":"","figs":["9.2-3.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"exam <- c(62, 65, 68, 70, 71, 72, 73, 74, 75, 76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 82, 83, 85, 87, 90, 94)\nresp <- c(1.2, 1.4, 1.5, 1.7, 1.9, 2.0, 2.2, 2.3, 2.6, 2.8, 3.1, 3.5, 3.9, 4.6, 5.4, 6.8, 8.9, 12.5, 18.7, 31.2)\nshapiro.test(exam)\nshapiro.test(resp)\npar(mfrow = c(1, 2))\nqqnorm(exam, main = \"Exam scores\"); qqline(exam)\nqqnorm(resp, main = \"Response times\"); qqline(resp)","expect":"exam: W = 0.993, p-value = 0.9998 (approx); response times: W = 0.641, p-value = 8.2e-06 (below 0.0001)","note":"shapiro.test prints W and the p-value; the Q-Q plots are drawn by qqnorm plus qqline.","explain":["Store the 20 exam scores.","Store the 20 response times in seconds.","shapiro.test runs the Shapiro-Wilk test of normality on the exam scores.","Run the same test on the response times.","Set up the plot window with 1 row and 2 columns of panels.","Q-Q plot of exam scores; qqline adds the reference line. The semicolon lets both commands share a line.","Q-Q plot of response times with its reference line."],"read":"The first test gives W = 0.993 and p-value about 0.9998: no evidence against normality. The second gives W = 0.641 and a tiny p-value of about 8e-06: strong evidence of non-normality. In the Q-Q plots the exam scores sit on the line and the response times curve away."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore (Plots: Normality plots with tests)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / exam resp.\nBEGIN DATA\n62 1.2  65 1.4  68 1.5  70 1.7  71 1.9  72 2.0  73 2.2  74 2.3  75 2.6  76 2.8\n77 3.1  78 3.5  79 3.9  80 4.6  82 5.4  83 6.8  85 8.9  87 12.5  90 18.7  94 31.2\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=exam resp\n  /PLOT=NPPLOT\n  /STATISTICS=NONE.","note":"The Tests of Normality table shows Shapiro-Wilk (statistic and Sig.) and also Kolmogorov-Smirnov; use the Shapiro-Wilk row. NPPLOT adds the normal Q-Q plots.","explain":["Declare the two variables to read in free format: exam and resp.","Marks the start of the data typed into the syntax.","The first ten students, each as an exam score then a response time.","The last ten, in the same layout.","Marks the end of the data.","EXAMINE explores the variables exam and resp.","NPPLOT requests normal Q-Q plots and the tests of normality.","STATISTICS=NONE switches off the other summary tables so only the normality output remains."],"read":"In Tests of Normality use the Shapiro-Wilk columns: exam has Statistic .993 and Sig. .9998 (no evidence against normality); resp has Statistic .641 and Sig. .000 (strong evidence against). The Normal Q-Q Plots show the same: exam on the line, resp curving away."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 20\ngenerate exam = real(word(\"62 65 68 70 71 72 73 74 75 76 77 78 79 80 82 83 85 87 90 94\", _n))\ngenerate resp = real(word(\"1.2 1.4 1.5 1.7 1.9 2.0 2.2 2.3 2.6 2.8 3.1 3.5 3.9 4.6 5.4 6.8 8.9 12.5 18.7 31.2\", _n))\nswilk exam resp\nqnorm exam\nqnorm resp","note":"swilk prints W and V statistics plus a z and Prob>z; the Prob>z column is the p-value (it is a close approximation to the others, and the Shapiro-Wilk W matches). qnorm draws a normal Q-Q plot.","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Create 20 empty rows.","Fill the exam scores. word() picks the n-th number from the quoted list and real() turns it into a number.","Fill the response times the same way.","swilk runs the Shapiro-Wilk test of normality on both variables.","qnorm draws the normal Q-Q plot for exam.","qnorm draws the normal Q-Q plot for resp."],"read":"In the swilk table read W and the Prob>z column: exam has W = 0.993 and Prob>z near 0.9 (no evidence against normality); resp has W = 0.641 and Prob>z near 0.00001 (strong evidence against). In the qnorm plots exam follows the line and resp curves away."},"conclusion":"The exam scores look normal enough for a t-test (Shapiro-Wilk p = 0.9998, dots on the Q-Q line). The response times do not: W = 0.641, p < 0.0001, a long right tail, and mean 5.91 against median 2.95. For response times try a transformation or a rank-based test. Caution: with only 20 values the tests have little power, so also look at the plots.","practice":{"scenario":"An economist has two samples of 15 values each: adult heights in centimetres and monthly incomes in thousands of dollars. Which one looks normal? If a sample is skewed, does taking the logarithm fix it?","data":"heights = [158, 161, 163, 164, 166, 167, 168, 169, 170, 171, 173, 174, 176, 178, 181]\nincome = [21, 23, 24, 26, 27, 29, 31, 34, 38, 45, 52, 66, 90, 140, 260]","tasks":["Run Shapiro-Wilk on heights and on income and report W and p.","Compare the mean and the median of each.","Run Shapiro-Wilk on the logarithm of income. Is it fixed?"],"hint":"Use stats.shapiro on each sample and on np.log(income). A mean far above the median hints at right skew.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nheights = np.array([158, 161, 163, 164, 166, 167, 168, 169, 170, 171, 173, 174, 176, 178, 181])\nincome = np.array([21, 23, 24, 26, 27, 29, 31, 34, 38, 45, 52, 66, 90, 140, 260])\nfor name, x in [('heights', heights), ('income', income), ('log income', np.log(income))]:\n    w = stats.shapiro(x)\n    print(f'{name:11s} W = {w.statistic:.3f}, p = {w.pvalue:.4f}, mean = {x.mean():.1f}, median = {np.median(x):.1f}')","answer":"Heights: W = 0.993, p = 0.9999, mean 169.3 and median 169.0, so they look normal. Income: W = 0.639, p = 0.0001, mean 60.4 against median 34.0, strongly right-skewed. After taking logs, W rises to 0.864 and p = 0.0272, much better but still below 0.05, so the log helps but does not fully fix this extreme skew. A rank-based test may be safer.","out":"heights     W = 0.993, p = 0.9999, mean = 169.3, median = 169.0\nincome      W = 0.639, p = 0.0001, mean = 60.4, median = 34.0\nlog income  W = 0.864, p = 0.0272, mean = 3.8, median = 3.5","figs":[]}}});
